window.CET4_BANKS.push({"id": "2025-06-3", "source": "2025年06月真题·第3套", "writing": {"prompt": "Directions: Suppose your university is organizing a forum on the development of students' cross-cultural communication abilities. You are now to write an essay to express your view. You will have 30 minutes to write the essay. You should write at least 120 words but no more than 180 words.", "sample": "In an era of deepening globalization, the ability to communicate across cultures has become indispensable. I\nfirmly believe that universities should attach great importance to developing students' cross-cultural\ncommunication abilities.\nFor one thing, such competence helps reduce misunderstandings rooted in different customs and values,\nmaking cooperation smoother. For instance, when I joined an international online project, teammates from\nthree countries reached agreement quickly only because we respected each other's communication styles. For\nanother, in today's competitive job market, graduates who can embrace diversity and work with global partners\nclearly stand out.\nTherefore, we students should seize every chance to broaden our horizons, such as joining exchange programs,\nreading foreign works, or making friends with international students. Only by becoming true global citizens\ncan we thrive in a connected world."}, "translation": {"prompt": "近年来，中国越来越多的城市着力打造\"15分钟便民生活圈(convenient living circles)\"。社区居民步行15分钟就能享受到日常所需的公共服务。生活圈内建有便利店、公园、健身场地、图书馆、学校、社区食堂、诊所等。生活圈的建立能够为居民提供更加便利、舒适、友好、愉悦的生活环境，更好地满足城市居民多元化的日常生活服务需求，提升居民的生活品质和幸福感。", "sample": "In recent years, an increasing number of Chinese cities have been striving to build \"15-minute convenient living circles\". Within such a circle, community residents can have access to the public services they need in daily life within just a 15-minute walk. There are facilities such as convenience stores, parks, fitness areas, libraries, schools, community canteens and clinics. The establishment of these living circles can provide residents with a more convenient, comfortable, friendly and pleasant living environment, thus better meeting the diverse daily-life service needs of urban residents and improving their quality of life and sense of happiness."}, "listeningUrl": null, "clozePassage": "The human brain has the ability to constantly form new networks and connections between brain cells. This means the brain has an almost magical ability to change. This characteristic is  __26__  apparent in a new study, focusing on people who underwent a hemispherectomy, a surgical procedure in which half of the brain is removed to treat illness.\nThe findings showed that, despite missing half of this critical  __27__  , participants could function well. This was because the  __28__  half of their brain strengthened. In fact, researchers found that connections and thus communication—between parts of different brain networks are actually stronger in people who had hemispherectomies than in people with intact brains. This  __29__  that the brain is able to compensate for loss of certain brain structure.\nThere are many other cases in the medical  __30__  that document the brain's amazing ability to  __31__  For example, a young boy had a significant portion of his brain removed for medical reasons, which included the part of the brain  __32__  for sight. But a few years after his surgery, scientists found that he could  __33__  see. They determined that this was because other parts of his brain took over the missing part's  __34__  tasks. Another study involved people who could smell, despite missing the region in the brain that processes information about smells. Though they are not sure how these individuals  __35__  the ability to smell, scientists believe it is possible that another part of the brain took on that task.", "matchIntro": "\"Phantom\" Traffic Jams Are Real—And Scientists Know How to Stop Them", "matchParagraphs": [{"label": "A", "text": "Traffic jams are a common problem of most big cities, either in developed or in developing countries, but one kind of traffic jams in particular can be especially frustrating. These are generally known as \"phantom\" traffic jams, in which dense traffic crawls to a halt for no apparent reason at all."}, {"label": "B", "text": "\"When caught in such a traffic jam, it is common for drivers to assume that there must be an accident, or construction or something,\" said Massachusetts Institute of Technology (MIT) computer science professor Berthold Horn. \"And then you drive and drive and drive… and there isn't anything there.\""}, {"label": "C", "text": "The exact cause of these phantom traffic jams has long remained a mystery. But now, new research has found the cause and researchers may have come up with a solution for this frustrating traffic pattern: maintaining equal spacing between cars on the road, rather than driving so closely behind another vehicle that a collision(碰撞) is likely if that vehicle brakes suddenly."}, {"label": "D", "text": "Horn said phantom traffic jams are an emergent property of the flow of vehicles down a highway. A phantom jam begins when a car in dense traffic slows down even slightly, which causes the car behind that vehicle to slow even more—and the slowing action spreads backward through the lane of traffic like a wave, getting worse the farther it spreads. Eventually, the cars far behind are forced to stop completely or risk hitting the slower vehicles ahead—and so the traffic grinds to a halt over nothing, Horn said."}, {"label": "E", "text": "Horn has been working on the problem of phantom traffic jams for years, and he has come up with a solution: By splitting the difference between the cars in front and the cars behind, the spacing of cars in a stream of traffic can act as a damper(抑制) on phantom traffic jams. That is, it can prevent the slow-down effect from being amplified(增强) by the cars that follow behind, he said. In essence, each driver would constantly make adjustments to keep their car roughly halfway between the car in front and the car behind."}, {"label": "F", "text": "Horn calls this method of vehicle spacing bilateral(双边的) control, and he said it could be achieved with relatively simple modifications to the adaptive cruise control that many new cars already have. Adaptive cruise control is a driver assistance technology that sets a maximum speed for vehicles and automatically slows the speed of the car when traffic is sensed in front of the vehicle. The technology is also known as autonomous cruise control. Such modifications, Horn said, could help fight phantom jams within a few years, maybe decades before an alternative solution, such as networked self-driving cars, becomes a reality on the roads."}, {"label": "G", "text": "Traffic jams are caused by a number of factors, such as roads that are poorly designed or over-capacity, localized factors like city traffic-lighting patterns, and events like accidents and construction. But phantom traffic jams can make all those problems worse, Horn said. And the researchers in the new study have calculated that solving phantom jams could save at least some of the estimated $121 billion per year that traffic jams are estimated to cost the U.S. economy, Horn told Live Science."}, {"label": "H", "text": "New research by Horn and MIT postdoctoral associate Liang Wang, published in the journal IEEE Transactions on Intelligent Transportation Systems, shows how bilateral control can fight the problem. The researchers made computer models of real-world traffic conditions, showing how a typical U.S. freeway could benefit from cars using bilateral control. That would be in place when human drivers fail to slow down, thus avoiding tailgating(紧跟) the car in front, Horn said. \"Under reasonable conditions today, you might get 1,800 cars per lane per hour throughput (the volume of traffic passing through),\" he added. \"But with bilateral control, you could almost double that.\""}, {"label": "I", "text": "The main alternative to increasing traffic throughput per lane has been to build more lanes and more highways, and putting in new roads is very expensive, he said. \"If we can increase the throughput on major highways, even if it's only by 50 percent, that would be a big deal,\" Horn said."}, {"label": "J", "text": "With the help of engineering students at high schools in Maine, Horn and Wang have also built a robot simulation(模拟) that shows how bilateral control can suppress phantom traffic jams. Increasing the spacing of vehicles dampens the effect of slowdowns by cars in front of a given car, so that the slowing is not worsened until it becomes a phantom jam, the researchers said."}, {"label": "K", "text": "A video from the project shows a train of autonomous robots running along a track without bilateral control; they eventually back up in phantom jams. But when the robots are switched over to bilateral control (by flashing the lights in the room), each autonomous robot tries to keep itself halfway between the robot in front and the one behind; the phantom jams disappear as a result, Horn said."}, {"label": "L", "text": "Since starting his research on phantom traffic jams, Horn has learned that schools of fish and flocks of birds and bats use similar spacing strategies to avoid hitting each other while swimming or flying in densely packed groups. In particular, studies of millions of bats emerging at dusk from caves in Southeast Asia showed that each bat used a form of bilateral control to reduce the likelihood of collisions with other bats in the densely packed swarm(一大群), Horn said."}, {"label": "M", "text": "Unlike bats, birds and fish, however, humans have difficulty judging distances behind them. To address that weakness, Horn is now working with the car company Toyota to enable a form of bilateral control via modifications to existing adaptive cruise control systems, which use forward-facing sensors to judge the distance to the vehicle ahead. The bilateral control approach would include adding rear-facing sensors to determine the distance to the vehicle following a car."}, {"label": "N", "text": "But until those systems become standard, human drivers can help reduce phantom traffic jams simply by not following the car in front so closely. As Horn argues, \"There's absolutely no advantage of tailgating the car in front of you.\""}], "readingPassages": {"46": "", "47": "", "48": "", "49": "", "50": "", "51": "Plant-based meats are coming soon to a dinner table near you, but do they deserve a spot on your plate? If you tried a vegetable burger years ago and dismissed it as rubbery and flavorless, it's a good time to give meatless meat another chance. Newer varieties imitate the look, flavor, and texture of meat. Analysts don't expect the meat-free movement to slow anytime soon; a 2021 report predicts the market will skyrocket to $74 billion by 2030—a 957 percent surge.\nPlant-based meats are no longer just for vegetarians. Companies such as Impossible Foods and Beyond Meat—the current superstars of the alternative protein sector—are pursuing consumers who enjoy meat but want to reduce their meat consumption for animal rights, health, or environmental reasons. So far, it's a winning strategy: More than nine out of ten consumers who buy Beyond Burger and Impossible Foods products also eat meat.\nMany vegetarians don't actually care for food that resembles meat. When reporter Joan Solsman, a longtime vegetarian, tried a dish from Impossible Foods, she felt so sick that she couldn't finish it. \"Maybe the best sign that Impossible Foods has cracked the code to realistic fake meat is that I couldn't stand to take another bite,\" she wrote.\nMeatless meats are generally a healthier choice than beef because they are cholesterol-free, plus they're a good source of vitamins, minerals, protein, and fiber.\nIf meatless meat can help you stick to a plant-based diet, that in itself can lead to better health. Not only has red meat been linked to cancer, but studies show that people who ditch meat have lower blood pressure, lower average blood sugar, and lower cholesterol levels.\nHowever, just because it's plant-based doesn't mean it's health food. Most imitation meats are highly processed and contain high amounts of sodium(钠) compared to traditional beef.\nThese new generations of alternate meat get all the attention, but don't forget about whole grains and vegetables. Beans are an especially excellent protein substitute. They're nutritious, inexpensive, and far more sustainable than any of the processed meatless substitutes on the market today.", "52": "Plant-based meats are coming soon to a dinner table near you, but do they deserve a spot on your plate? If you tried a vegetable burger years ago and dismissed it as rubbery and flavorless, it's a good time to give meatless meat another chance. Newer varieties imitate the look, flavor, and texture of meat. Analysts don't expect the meat-free movement to slow anytime soon; a 2021 report predicts the market will skyrocket to $74 billion by 2030—a 957 percent surge.\nPlant-based meats are no longer just for vegetarians. Companies such as Impossible Foods and Beyond Meat—the current superstars of the alternative protein sector—are pursuing consumers who enjoy meat but want to reduce their meat consumption for animal rights, health, or environmental reasons. So far, it's a winning strategy: More than nine out of ten consumers who buy Beyond Burger and Impossible Foods products also eat meat.\nMany vegetarians don't actually care for food that resembles meat. When reporter Joan Solsman, a longtime vegetarian, tried a dish from Impossible Foods, she felt so sick that she couldn't finish it. \"Maybe the best sign that Impossible Foods has cracked the code to realistic fake meat is that I couldn't stand to take another bite,\" she wrote.\nMeatless meats are generally a healthier choice than beef because they are cholesterol-free, plus they're a good source of vitamins, minerals, protein, and fiber.\nIf meatless meat can help you stick to a plant-based diet, that in itself can lead to better health. Not only has red meat been linked to cancer, but studies show that people who ditch meat have lower blood pressure, lower average blood sugar, and lower cholesterol levels.\nHowever, just because it's plant-based doesn't mean it's health food. Most imitation meats are highly processed and contain high amounts of sodium(钠) compared to traditional beef.\nThese new generations of alternate meat get all the attention, but don't forget about whole grains and vegetables. Beans are an especially excellent protein substitute. They're nutritious, inexpensive, and far more sustainable than any of the processed meatless substitutes on the market today.", "53": "Plant-based meats are coming soon to a dinner table near you, but do they deserve a spot on your plate? If you tried a vegetable burger years ago and dismissed it as rubbery and flavorless, it's a good time to give meatless meat another chance. Newer varieties imitate the look, flavor, and texture of meat. Analysts don't expect the meat-free movement to slow anytime soon; a 2021 report predicts the market will skyrocket to $74 billion by 2030—a 957 percent surge.\nPlant-based meats are no longer just for vegetarians. Companies such as Impossible Foods and Beyond Meat—the current superstars of the alternative protein sector—are pursuing consumers who enjoy meat but want to reduce their meat consumption for animal rights, health, or environmental reasons. So far, it's a winning strategy: More than nine out of ten consumers who buy Beyond Burger and Impossible Foods products also eat meat.\nMany vegetarians don't actually care for food that resembles meat. When reporter Joan Solsman, a longtime vegetarian, tried a dish from Impossible Foods, she felt so sick that she couldn't finish it. \"Maybe the best sign that Impossible Foods has cracked the code to realistic fake meat is that I couldn't stand to take another bite,\" she wrote.\nMeatless meats are generally a healthier choice than beef because they are cholesterol-free, plus they're a good source of vitamins, minerals, protein, and fiber.\nIf meatless meat can help you stick to a plant-based diet, that in itself can lead to better health. Not only has red meat been linked to cancer, but studies show that people who ditch meat have lower blood pressure, lower average blood sugar, and lower cholesterol levels.\nHowever, just because it's plant-based doesn't mean it's health food. Most imitation meats are highly processed and contain high amounts of sodium(钠) compared to traditional beef.\nThese new generations of alternate meat get all the attention, but don't forget about whole grains and vegetables. Beans are an especially excellent protein substitute. They're nutritious, inexpensive, and far more sustainable than any of the processed meatless substitutes on the market today.", "54": "Plant-based meats are coming soon to a dinner table near you, but do they deserve a spot on your plate? If you tried a vegetable burger years ago and dismissed it as rubbery and flavorless, it's a good time to give meatless meat another chance. Newer varieties imitate the look, flavor, and texture of meat. Analysts don't expect the meat-free movement to slow anytime soon; a 2021 report predicts the market will skyrocket to $74 billion by 2030—a 957 percent surge.\nPlant-based meats are no longer just for vegetarians. Companies such as Impossible Foods and Beyond Meat—the current superstars of the alternative protein sector—are pursuing consumers who enjoy meat but want to reduce their meat consumption for animal rights, health, or environmental reasons. So far, it's a winning strategy: More than nine out of ten consumers who buy Beyond Burger and Impossible Foods products also eat meat.\nMany vegetarians don't actually care for food that resembles meat. When reporter Joan Solsman, a longtime vegetarian, tried a dish from Impossible Foods, she felt so sick that she couldn't finish it. \"Maybe the best sign that Impossible Foods has cracked the code to realistic fake meat is that I couldn't stand to take another bite,\" she wrote.\nMeatless meats are generally a healthier choice than beef because they are cholesterol-free, plus they're a good source of vitamins, minerals, protein, and fiber.\nIf meatless meat can help you stick to a plant-based diet, that in itself can lead to better health. Not only has red meat been linked to cancer, but studies show that people who ditch meat have lower blood pressure, lower average blood sugar, and lower cholesterol levels.\nHowever, just because it's plant-based doesn't mean it's health food. Most imitation meats are highly processed and contain high amounts of sodium(钠) compared to traditional beef.\nThese new generations of alternate meat get all the attention, but don't forget about whole grains and vegetables. Beans are an especially excellent protein substitute. They're nutritious, inexpensive, and far more sustainable than any of the processed meatless substitutes on the market today.", "55": "Plant-based meats are coming soon to a dinner table near you, but do they deserve a spot on your plate? If you tried a vegetable burger years ago and dismissed it as rubbery and flavorless, it's a good time to give meatless meat another chance. Newer varieties imitate the look, flavor, and texture of meat. Analysts don't expect the meat-free movement to slow anytime soon; a 2021 report predicts the market will skyrocket to $74 billion by 2030—a 957 percent surge.\nPlant-based meats are no longer just for vegetarians. Companies such as Impossible Foods and Beyond Meat—the current superstars of the alternative protein sector—are pursuing consumers who enjoy meat but want to reduce their meat consumption for animal rights, health, or environmental reasons. So far, it's a winning strategy: More than nine out of ten consumers who buy Beyond Burger and Impossible Foods products also eat meat.\nMany vegetarians don't actually care for food that resembles meat. When reporter Joan Solsman, a longtime vegetarian, tried a dish from Impossible Foods, she felt so sick that she couldn't finish it. \"Maybe the best sign that Impossible Foods has cracked the code to realistic fake meat is that I couldn't stand to take another bite,\" she wrote.\nMeatless meats are generally a healthier choice than beef because they are cholesterol-free, plus they're a good source of vitamins, minerals, protein, and fiber.\nIf meatless meat can help you stick to a plant-based diet, that in itself can lead to better health. Not only has red meat been linked to cancer, but studies show that people who ditch meat have lower blood pressure, lower average blood sugar, and lower cholesterol levels.\nHowever, just because it's plant-based doesn't mean it's health food. Most imitation meats are highly processed and contain high amounts of sodium(钠) compared to traditional beef.\nThese new generations of alternate meat get all the attention, but don't forget about whole grains and vegetables. Beans are an especially excellent protein substitute. They're nutritious, inexpensive, and far more sustainable than any of the processed meatless substitutes on the market today."}, "questions": [{"id": "2025-06-3-c-26", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 26, "stem": "", "options": ["A) adapt", "B) hardly", "C) indicates", "D) insist", "E) literature", "F) organ", "G) particularly", "H) remaining", "I) responsible", "J) retained", "K) still", "L) strategy", "M) transformed", "N) underlines", "O) visual"], "answer": "G", "analysis": "【答案】G) particularly\n副词\n【词性槽】is __ apparent 系表结构里修饰形容词\n【依据】‘尤其明显’才与下文的研究举例语气一致\nThis characteristic is __(26)__ apparent in a new study接的是一项新研究做例证，常见说\n法是‘这一特性在……中尤为明显’；particularly apparent 是固定的强调搭配。\n【竞争词】B) hardly hardly 是否定副词，与全段‘强调可塑性’相反\nhardly apparent 意为‘几乎不明显’，会把全篇‘大脑可塑性惊人’的论点一下子否掉，逻辑上正话\n反说。\nK) still still 此处会变成‘仍然明显’，弱化强调\nstill apparent 表‘照旧明显’，没问题但语气平淡；本句要的是‘举新研究来凸显’的强调感，比\nparticularly 弱；且 still 在第 33 题‘he could __ see’里有更强的搭配需求（hardly/still see 的\n二选一）。", "points": ["选词填空", "副词"]}, {"id": "2025-06-3-c-27", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 27, "stem": "", "options": ["A) adapt", "B) hardly", "C) indicates", "D) insist", "E) literature", "F) organ", "G) particularly", "H) remaining", "I) responsible", "J) retained", "K) still", "L) strategy", "M) transformed", "N) underlines", "O) visual"], "answer": "F", "analysis": "【答案】F) organ\n名词\n【词性槽】this+形容词+__ 空缺名词中心词\n【依据】‘大脑’本身就是 critical organ（关键器官）\n上文反复在讲 brain（大脑），代词 this 回指 brain，而大脑正是人体最关键的器官之一，\nmissing half of this critical __(27)__填 organ 后语义自洽：缺失了这个关键器官的一半。\n【竞争词】E) literature literature 指文献，不能被‘半个’切\nliterature 是抽象名词‘文献/文学’，跟 half of this critical 搭不上——不能说‘缺了关键文献的一\n半’；它真正的用武之地在第 30 题‘medical literature 医学文献’那种搭配里。\nL) strategy strategy 指策略，与 brain 的指代对不上\nthis 在语篇里回指上文提到的实体，而上文核心实体是 brain（一个具体器官），不是某种策\n略；且 missing half of a strategy 也不是地道说法。", "points": ["选词填空", "名词"]}, {"id": "2025-06-3-c-28", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 28, "stem": "", "options": ["A) adapt", "B) hardly", "C) indicates", "D) insist", "E) literature", "F) organ", "G) particularly", "H) remaining", "I) responsible", "J) retained", "K) still", "L) strategy", "M) transformed", "N) underlines", "O) visual"], "answer": "H", "analysis": "【答案】H) remaining\n形容词\n【词性槽】the+__+half 中间缺修饰名词的形容词\n【依据】切了一半 → 强壮起来的是‘剩下的’那一半\n上一题刚说 missing half of this critical organ（缺了一半），那么能 strengthened 的当\n然是‘另一半，剩下的’，the __(28)__ half of their brain strengthened填 remaining 与上\n文形成‘缺一半—剩一半补偿’的呼应。\n【竞争词】I) responsible responsible 不能直接作前置定语修饰 half\nresponsible 表‘负有责任的’，习惯用法是 be responsible for…，几乎不直接前置修饰像 half 这\n种抽象量词；语义上也与‘剩下的那一半’对不上。\nO) visual ‘视觉的一半大脑’语义无所指\nvisual 是‘视觉的’，本段并未把话题缩到视觉脑区，下文谈视觉补偿是在第三段男孩例子里；放\n在此处会把全段主旨从‘整体半脑代偿’偏到‘视觉’上。", "points": ["选词填空", "形容词"]}, {"id": "2025-06-3-c-29", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 29, "stem": "", "options": ["A) adapt", "B) hardly", "C) indicates", "D) insist", "E) literature", "F) organ", "G) particularly", "H) remaining", "I) responsible", "J) retained", "K) still", "L) strategy", "M) transformed", "N) underlines", "O) visual"], "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C) indicates\n动词\n【词性槽】This __ that… 主语+谓语+宾语从句缺谓语\n【依据】‘上文现象→表明这一推论’，indicates 最中性贴切\nThis __(29)__ that the brain is able to compensate for loss of certain brain structure\n是用上文‘连接更强’的事实推出‘大脑能代偿’这一结论，indicates that…（表明/说明……）是\n英语里把现象引向结论的最标准说法。\n【竞争词】N) underlines underlines 偏‘强调/凸显已成共识的事’，本句是‘推论’不是‘强调旧识’\nunderline that 也能接从句，但语气是‘再次强调’，前提是该结论早已被反复说过；本句是基于\n上文新证据‘得出’结论，用 indicates（表明/暗示）更合逻辑流，且 underlines 偏文体强烈。\n【易错】最具迷惑性的错误词是 N) —— underlines 与 indicates 都能接 that 从句、词性时态全过\n关。This __(29)__ that the brain is able to compensate for loss of certain brain\nstructure里，主体考生一看 underlines that 也通就容易选错；防坑要看上文是‘新发现’还\n是‘旧结论’，本句是新发现支撑推论，用 indicates 才对。", "points": ["选词填空", "动词"]}, {"id": "2025-06-3-c-30", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 30, "stem": "", "options": ["A) adapt", "B) hardly", "C) indicates", "D) insist", "E) literature", "F) organ", "G) particularly", "H) remaining", "I) responsible", "J) retained", "K) still", "L) strategy", "M) transformed", "N) underlines", "O) visual"], "answer": "E", "analysis": "【答案】E) literature\n名词\n【词性槽】in the medical __ 介词+形容词后的名词槽\n【依据】medical literature 是固定搭配‘医学文献’\nThere are many other cases in the medical __(30)__ that document the brain's\namazing ability意为‘医学文献里有很多其他案例记录了大脑的惊人能力’，medical\nliterature（医学文献）是英语里讲学术案例来源的标准搭配。\n【竞争词】L) strategy medical strategy 是‘医疗策略’，与 cases that document 对不上\nstrategy 指策略/方略，而本句 cases that document（记录案例的）只能存在于文献/记录里，\n不能存在于‘策略’里；逻辑上 in the medical strategy 也偏怪。", "points": ["选词填空", "名词"]}, {"id": "2025-06-3-c-31", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 31, "stem": "", "options": ["A) adapt", "B) hardly", "C) indicates", "D) insist", "E) literature", "F) organ", "G) particularly", "H) remaining", "I) responsible", "J) retained", "K) still", "L) strategy", "M) transformed", "N) underlines", "O) visual"], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A) adapt\n动词\n【词性槽】ability to __ 不定式 to 后接动词原形\n【依据】全篇主旨就是‘大脑能适应/代偿’\n回到 overview：the brain is able to compensate for loss of certain brain structure\n——‘代偿’与‘适应’语义同向；the brain's amazing ability to __(31)__填 adapt，意即‘大脑\n惊人的适应能力’，与全篇主旨完全对齐。\n【竞争词】D) insist insist 表‘坚持主张’，主语是大脑不通\ninsist 多用于人的‘坚持要求/坚持认为’，跟无生命的 brain 搭配语义怪异；且与下文举的两个例\n子（视觉代偿、嗅觉代偿）无关，例证是用来证明‘适应/代偿’而非‘坚持’。", "points": ["选词填空", "动词"]}, {"id": "2025-06-3-c-32", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 32, "stem": "", "options": ["A) adapt", "B) hardly", "C) indicates", "D) insist", "E) literature", "F) organ", "G) particularly", "H) remaining", "I) responsible", "J) retained", "K) still", "L) strategy", "M) transformed", "N) underlines", "O) visual"], "answer": "I", "analysis": "【答案】I) responsible\n形容词\n【词性槽】the part of the brain __ for sight 缺后置定语形容词\n【依据】固定搭配 be responsible for 锁死\n看见 __ for 这种‘形容词+for’框架，几乎立刻想到 responsible for（负责……的）；the\npart of the brain __(32)__ for sight即‘负责视觉的那部分大脑’，是英语里讲脑区功能的标\n准说法。\n【竞争词】O) visual visual 不与 for 搭配，且会和 sight 语义打架\nvisual 是‘视觉的’，没有 visual for… 的搭配；同时 visual 本身就含‘视觉’的意思，再加 for sight\n就成了‘视觉的为了视力’，重复又不通。", "points": ["选词填空", "形容词"]}, {"id": "2025-06-3-c-33", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 33, "stem": "", "options": ["A) adapt", "B) hardly", "C) indicates", "D) insist", "E) literature", "F) organ", "G) particularly", "H) remaining", "I) responsible", "J) retained", "K) still", "L) strategy", "M) transformed", "N) underlines", "O) visual"], "answer": "K", "analysis": "【答案】K) still\n副词\n【词性槽】could __ see 情态动词与原形动词之间缺副词\n【依据】“切了视觉脑区还是能看见”才呼应代偿主旨\na young boy had a significant portion of his brain removed for medical reasons,\nwhich included the part of the brain __(32)__ for sight. But a few years after his\nsurgery, scientists found that he could __(33)__ see.——But+几年后+发现他能看见，\n要的是‘竟然还能看见’这种转折后肯定，still see 正合此意，且呼应主旨‘大脑代偿’。\n【竞争词】B) hardly hardly see 是‘几乎看不见’，与 But… 转折后的肯定语气反向\nhardly 是半否定副词，会把句意变成‘他几乎看不见’，那就和第三段‘大脑居然能代偿’的全段定调\n彻底相反。看到 But + scientists found 的‘惊喜发现’语气，要的就是肯定的‘still’。\n【易错】最具迷惑性的错误词是 B) —— hardly 是‘最熟脸’副词，最容易误填进 could __ see。he\ncould __(33)__ see里 could hardly see 读着也通顺，但忽视了上文 But+几年后\n+scientists found 的‘惊喜发现’语气；防坑：见 But 引出的肯定发现，副词槽几乎必选肯定\n型（still/even），半否定（hardly/scarcely）几乎一律错。", "points": ["选词填空", "副词"]}, {"id": "2025-06-3-c-34", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 34, "stem": "", "options": ["A) adapt", "B) hardly", "C) indicates", "D) insist", "E) literature", "F) organ", "G) particularly", "H) remaining", "I) responsible", "J) retained", "K) still", "L) strategy", "M) transformed", "N) underlines", "O) visual"], "answer": "O", "analysis": "【答案】O) visual\n形容词\n【词性槽】the missing part's __ tasks 缺修饰 tasks 的形容词\n【依据】上文是‘负责视觉的脑区’，被接管的自然是视觉任务\n32 题已说 the part of the brain responsible for sight，34 题接 the missing part's __\ntasks——既然 missing part 负责的是 sight，那它的 tasks 就是 visual tasks（视觉任\n务），语义闭环。", "points": ["选词填空", "形容词"]}, {"id": "2025-06-3-c-35", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 35, "stem": "", "options": ["A) adapt", "B) hardly", "C) indicates", "D) insist", "E) literature", "F) organ", "G) particularly", "H) remaining", "I) responsible", "J) retained", "K) still", "L) strategy", "M) transformed", "N) underlines", "O) visual"], "answer": "J", "analysis": "【答案】J) retained\n动词\n【词性槽】how these individuals __ the ability 从句缺谓语动词\n【依据】‘没了脑区却仍保有嗅觉能力’，retained 精准对位\npeople who could smell, despite missing the region in the brain that processes\ninformation about smells. Though they are not sure how these individuals __(35)__\nthe ability to smell——despite missing… 与 retained the ability（保留了能力）形成完\n美让步对照：脑区缺了，能力还在‘保留着’，正是‘代偿’的典型描述。\n【竞争词】M) transformed transformed 是‘改造/转变’，跟 the ability to smell 搭配不通\ntransform 通常表‘把 A 改造为 B（transform A into B）’，跟‘嗅觉能力’搭配会变成‘改造嗅觉能\n力’，与上下文‘他们居然还能闻得到’的‘保留’语义反向。", "points": ["选词填空", "动词"]}, {"id": "2025-06-3-m-36", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 36, "stem": "The age-long mystery has now been resolved as to how phantom traffic jams are caused.", "options": null, "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】谜题已解\n【定位】The exact cause of these phantom traffic jams has long remained a mystery. But now, new\nresearch has found the cause\n这种幻影堵车的确切成因长期以来一直是个谜。但如今，新研究已经找到了病因。\n【改写】has now been resolved ↔ new research has found the cause\n解决=找到病因", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2025-06-3-m-37", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 37, "stem": "It is possible to nearly double the hourly throughput per lane by adopting bilateral control.", "options": null, "answer": "H", "analysis": "【答案】H 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】通行量翻倍\n【定位】\"Under reasonable conditions today, you might get 1,800 cars per lane per hour throughput\n(the volume of traffic passing through),\" he added. \"But with bilateral control, you could\nalmost double that.\"\n“在今天的合理条件下，每车道每小时通行量大约 1800 辆，”他补充道。“但用双边控制，你\n几乎能让它翻倍。”\n【改写】nearly double the hourly throughput per lane ↔ you could almost double that\nnearly≈almost\n【辨邻】 I I 段说的是 increase the throughput on major highways, even if it's only by 50\npercent——靠修路只提升 50%，并非靠双边控制翻倍，与陈述句的“几乎翻倍”不符。\nIf we can increase the throughput on major highways, even if it's only by 50 percent", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2025-06-3-m-38", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 38, "stem": "According to Horn, drivers making constant adjustments for equal spacing between cars in a flow of traffic can help reduce phantom traffic jams.", "options": null, "answer": "E", "analysis": "【答案】E 段（弱锚点）\n【锚点】不断调整保持等距\n【定位】In essence, each driver would constantly make adjustments to keep their car roughly halfway\nbetween the car in front and the car behind.\n本质上，每个司机都要不断调整，让自己的车大致保持在前车与后车的正中间。\n【改写】making constant adjustments for equal spacing between cars ↔\nconstantly make adjustments to keep their car roughly halfway\n等距即居中\n【辨邻】 C C 段 maintaining equal spacing between cars on the road 只是概括性地点出解法\n叫“保持等距”，并没有讲“司机不断调整保持居中”这一具体动作，落点比 E 段浅。\nmaintaining equal spacing between cars on the road", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2025-06-3-m-39", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 39, "stem": "Before systems for bilateral control are installed, drivers should not follow the car in front too closely so as to reduce phantom traffic jams.", "options": null, "answer": "N", "analysis": "【答案】N 段（弱锚点）\n【锚点】系统普及前别紧跟\n【定位】But until those systems become standard, human drivers can help reduce phantom traffic jams\nsimply by not following the car in front so closely.\n但在这些系统普及之前，人类司机只要不那么紧跟前车，就能帮助减少幻影堵车。\n【改写】should not follow the car in front too closely ↔ not following the car in front so closely\ntoo closely≈so closely\n【辨邻】 F F 段 it could be achieved with relatively simple modifications to the adaptive\ncruise control 讲的是技术如何实现，而非“普及前司机的行为建议”，答非本句所问。\nit could be achieved with relatively simple modifications to the adaptive cruise control", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2025-06-3-m-40", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 40, "stem": "Horn said with some simple modifications to the existing equipment in new vehicles, their spacing bilateral control could be accomplished.", "options": null, "answer": "F", "analysis": "【答案】F 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】简单改装新车设备\n【定位】it could be achieved with relatively simple modifications to the adaptive cruise control that\nmany new cars already have.\n它只需对许多新车已经配备的自适应巡航控制做相对简单的改装就能实现。\n【改写】the existing equipment in new vehicles ↔\nthe adaptive cruise control that many new cars already have\n上位概括\n【辨邻】 M M 段 modifications to existing adaptive cruise control systems 的落点是“为解决\n人判断后方距离的弱点而加装后向传感器”（对应第 42 题），而非本句“简单改装即可\n实现”这一概括，重点不同。\nmodifications to existing adaptive cruise control systems", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2025-06-3-m-41", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 41, "stem": "Drivers caught up in phantom traffic jams are likely to think an accident has happened or some construction is going on.", "options": null, "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】以为事故或施工\n【定位】When caught in such a traffic jam, it is common for drivers to assume that there must be an\naccident, or construction or something\n被堵在这种车流里时，司机通常会以为前面一定出了事故，或者在施工之类。\n【改写】think an accident has happened or some construction is going on ↔\nassume that there must be an accident, or construction or something\nthink≈assume", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2025-06-3-m-42", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 42, "stem": "Two sets of sensors would be used to help drivers determine the distances between cars.", "options": null, "answer": "M", "analysis": "【答案】M 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】两组传感器\n【定位】which use forward-facing sensors to judge the distance to the vehicle ahead. The bilateral\ncontrol approach would include adding rear-facing sensors to determine the distance to the\nvehicle following a car.\n（这些系统）用前向传感器判断与前车的距离。双边控制方案还要加装后向传感器，以测定\n与后方跟随车辆的距离。\n【改写】Two sets of sensors ↔ adding rear-facing sensors\n前向已有+后向新增=两组", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2025-06-3-m-43", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 43, "stem": "Solving the problem of phantom jams could save America billions of dollars every year.", "options": null, "answer": "G", "analysis": "【答案】G 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】每年省下数十亿\n【定位】solving phantom jams could save at least some of the estimated $121 billion per year that\ntraffic jams are estimated to cost the U.S. economy\n解决幻影堵车，能省下堵车每年给美国经济造成的、估计达 1210 亿美元损失中的至少一\n部分。\n【改写】save America billions of dollars every year ↔\nsave at least some of the estimated $121 billion per year\nbillions 概括 $121 billion\n【辨邻】 I I 段 putting in new roads is very expensive 讲的是替代方案花钱多，与陈述句“为美\n国每年省钱”方向相反。\nputting in new roads is very expensive", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2025-06-3-m-44", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 44, "stem": "In a phantom traffic jam, the cars in the same lane slow down one after another, creating a wave-like effect.", "options": null, "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】逐车减速的波浪效应\n【定位】the slowing action spreads backward through the lane of traffic like a wave, getting worse the\nfarther it spreads.\n减速这一动作像波浪一样沿车道向后蔓延，传得越远越严重。\n【改写】slow down one after another, creating a wave-like effect ↔\nthe slowing action spreads backward through the lane of traffic like a wave\n蔓延=逐车", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2025-06-3-m-45", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 45, "stem": "Horn has found that certain creatures use strategies similar to bilateral control to avoid hitting each other while moving in dense crowds.", "options": null, "answer": "L", "analysis": "【答案】L 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】动物用类似策略防撞\n【定位】schools of fish and flocks of birds and bats use similar spacing strategies to avoid hitting each\nother while swimming or flying in densely packed groups.\n鱼群、鸟群和蝙蝠会用类似的间距策略，避免在密集群体中游动或飞行时相互碰撞。\n【改写】use strategies similar to bilateral control to avoid hitting each other ↔\nuse similar spacing strategies to avoid hitting each other\n上位词替换", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2025-06-3-r-46", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 46, "stem": "What do we learn about American society from the first paragraph?", "options": ["A) It sees lots of personal income wasted on beauty products.", "B) It considers one's physical appearance very important.", "C) It places a high value on the physical fitness of stars.", "D) It expects every individual to look their very best."], "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B （细节题）\n【判型】What do we learn about American society from the first paragraph? 是 what...learn\n型客观信息问答，且范围锁定首段，属于典型细节题。\n【拆句】Q46 主干是 What do we learn about American society，from the first paragraph 是\n状语限定信息来源；句子平铺直叙、无陷阱。\n【定位】Our society places a high value on physical beauty.\n我们的社会高度看重外貌。\n【选项】A) 无中生有\nIt sees lots of personal income wasted on beauty products. 把 over $722 each\nyear on their appearance 的客观花费篡改为 \"被浪费\"，原文从未做此评价。\nB) ✓ 正确\nplaces a high value on physical beauty ↔\nconsiders one's physical appearance very important\n预判 \"美国社会把外貌看得很重要\" 与 considers one's physical appearance very\nimportant 字面贴合，无需推断。\nC) 偷换概念\nplaces a high value on the physical fitness of stars 把 \"看重外貌\" 的主体悄悄换成\n\"明星的 physical fitness\"；原文 One in ten Americans has tried to look like a star\n只说有人想模仿明星，未谈明星本身的体魄。\nD) 过度推断\nIt expects every individual to look their very best. 把 Our society places a high\nvalue on physical beauty 升级为 \"要求每一个人都做到最好\"，强度与范围都被拔高。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2025-06-3-r-47", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 47, "stem": "What happens when people strive to look better?", "options": ["A) They have to get prepared for resentment and bitter court battles.", "B) They are better able to gain attention, love, and personal connection.", "C) They may neglect to cultivate the inner qualities that matter more.", "D) They feel much of their precious time, energy and resources is wasted."], "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C （细节题）\n【判型】What happens when people strive to look better? 询问某动作引发的客观后果，是典型\n的因果型细节题。\n【拆句】Q47 主干 What happens 后接 when 引导的时间状语从句，提示发生条件；结构简单无\n陷阱。\n【定位】Many of us spend far too much time, energy, and money trying to polish an image of what we\nthink will bring attention, love, and connection. In the process, we may fail to attend to\nourselves in a way that would move us toward deeper intimacy, fulfillment, and meaning.\n我们当中许多人花了过多的时间、精力和金钱去打磨一个我们以为会带来关注、爱与连结的\n形象。在这个过程中，我们可能会忽略对自己的关照，错过本可带来更深亲密、满足与意义\n的方式。\n【选项】A) 张冠李戴\nThey have to get prepared for resentment and bitter court battles. 把第 4 段说明\n影星案例的 resentment and bitter court battles 强行移植到 Q47 的情境中，但原文\n从未把这描述为追求外表的 \"必然准备\"。\nB) 与原文相反\nThey are better able to gain attention, love, and personal connection. 把原文\nwhat we think will bring attention, love, and connection 中 \"我们以为会带来\" 的主\n观期待包装成客观事实，方向恰好与作者批评意图相反。\nC) ✓ 正确\nfail to attend to ourselves in a way that would move us toward deeper intimacy, fulfillment, and\nmeaning\n↔ neglect to cultivate the inner qualities that matter more\n预判 \"疏于关照内在更要紧的部分\" 几乎逐字对应 neglect to cultivate the inner\nqualities that matter more。\nD) 偷换概念\nThey feel much of their precious time, energy and resources is wasted. 把作者对\ntime, energy, and money 的客观描述换成 \"他们自己觉得被浪费\"，主体变成 they\nfeel；原文未提及当事人有此自觉。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2025-06-3-r-48", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 48, "stem": "What does the author say about outer beauty?", "options": ["A) It may be inherited or cultivated.", "B) It may lead to bad as well as good habits.", "C) It may create connection or isolation.", "D) It may do as much harm as good."], "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D （细节题）\n【判型】What does the author say about outer beauty? 询问作者就某概念的明确陈述，归入细\n节题。\n【拆句】Q48 主干 What does the author say，about outer beauty 是介词短语状语；无难句\n结构。\n【定位】Outer beauty can be as much of a curse as a blessing.\n外在美既可能是祝福，也同样可能是诅咒。\n【选项】A) 无中生有\nIt may be inherited or cultivated. 谈外貌的来源（遗传/后天培养），原文从未讨论外\n貌的获得方式。\nB) 偷换概念\nIt may lead to bad as well as good habits. 把 Outer beauty can be as much of a\ncurse as a blessing. 改成 \"导致好坏习惯\"，对象层面从 \"对人生整体的祸福\" 缩到 \"习\n惯\"。原文确实提到 develop a habit，但说的是 \"只关注外表的习惯\"，并非 \"好习惯/坏\n习惯\" 的对照。\nC) 与原文相反\nIt may create connection or isolation. 称外貌可能创造 connection；但 P5 P6 都\n说 connection 来自 inner qualities / meeting of inner worlds，外貌不直接创\n造 connection。\nD) ✓ 正确\nas much of a curse as a blessing ↔ do as much harm as good\n预判 \"坏处与好处一样多\" 直接命中 do as much harm as good，无需推断。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2025-06-3-r-49", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 49, "stem": "What brings about spiritual connection?", "options": ["A) Meeting of minds.", "B) Attraction at first sight.", "C) Physical chemistry.", "D) Frequent interaction."], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A （细节题）\n【判型】What brings about spiritual connection? 询问导致某结果的原因，对应原文中一处明确\n的因→果叙述。\n【拆句】Q49 主干 What brings about spiritual connection，brings about=cause/lead to，是\n一个三个单词的简单疑问句。\n【定位】While our initial attraction may be based, in part, on physical chemistry, it is the meeting of\nour inner worlds that creates the lasting intimacy and spiritual connection for which we long.\n虽然最初的吸引可能在一定程度上源于身体的化学反应，但真正创造我们所渴求的长久亲密\n与精神连结的，是内在世界的相遇。\n【选项】A) ✓ 正确\nthe meeting of our inner worlds ↔ Meeting of minds.\n预判 \"内在世界相遇\" 几乎逐字对应 Meeting of minds.。\nB) 与原文相反\nAttraction at first sight. 对应 our initial attraction，但原文明确说这只是 \"in part\" 的\n初始吸引，并非 spiritual connection 的来源。\nC) 与原文相反\nPhysical chemistry. 直接抓取 physical chemistry 这一原词，但原文用 While 让步结\n构说明它只贡献 initial attraction，不是 spiritual connection 的来源。\nD) 无中生有\nFrequent interaction. 谈交往频率，原文从未提及 spiritual connection 来自频繁\n互动。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2025-06-3-r-50", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 50, "stem": "What happens when we take the path toward cultivating inner beauty?", "options": ["A) We may find a route toward other people's souls.", "B) We may be appreciated more by people around us.", "C) We will meet people who value us for who we are.", "D) We will be discouraged from revealing our true selves."], "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C （细节题）\n【判型】What happens when we take the path toward cultivating inner beauty? 仍是 what\nhappens when 的因果型问法，问的是文章对此动作所述的结果。\n【拆句】Q50 主干 What happens，when 从句中 take the path toward cultivating inner beauty\n用了介词短语作 path 的修饰，toward 后接动名词 cultivating，提示这是个比喻：把培养\n内在美比作走一条路。\n【定位】Eventually we'll find those compatible souls who appreciate us as we are.\n最终我们会遇到那些欣赏真实的我们的契合灵魂。\n【选项】A) 与原文相反\nWe may find a route toward other people's souls. 让 \"我们走向别人的灵魂\"；原文\nP7 描述的是别人走向真实的我们，方向与重点都不一致。\nB) 以偏概全\nWe may be appreciated more by people around us. 把 \"被那些契合的人欣赏\" 扩大\n为 \"被周围所有人更多地欣赏\"；原文紧前一句明说 Not everyone will see us and\nappreciate us，恰好否定 \"被周围人普遍更欣赏\"。\nC) ✓ 正确\nEventually we'll find those compatible souls who appreciate us as we are. ↔\nWe will meet people who value us for who we are.\n预判 \"最终遇到欣赏真实自我的人\" 与 We will meet people who value us for who we\nare. 几乎逐字对应。\nD) 与原文相反\nWe will be discouraged from revealing our true selves. 称我们会被打消展露真我的\n念头；但原文 if they don't, it is their loss, not ours 是鼓励继续展露自我，与选项方向\n相反。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2025-06-3-r-51", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 51, "stem": "What can we expect of plant-based meats?", "options": ["A) An increasing interest in their analyses.", "B) An enormous effort in their promotion.", "C) A huge boost in their consumption.", "D) A noticeable surge in their varieties."], "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C （细节题）\n【判型】What can we expect of plant-based meats? 没有出现 infer/imply/suggest，问的是一\n个文中已表述的预期结果，属于事实细节题。\n【拆句】What can we expect of plant-based meats? 结构是 expect sth of sb/sth（对……抱有什\n么期待/预期），of 后接被期待的对象。考生熟悉的 expect sth from sb 在此处换成 of，\n含义相近，不必紧张。\n【定位】Analysts don't expect the meat-free movement to slow anytime soon; a 2021 report predicts\nthe market will skyrocket to $74 billion by 2030—a 957 percent surge.\n分析师们不预期植物肉浪潮短期内会放缓；一份 2021 年的报告预测，到 2030 年该市场将\n猛增至 740 亿美元——增幅高达 957%。\n【选项】A) 偷换概念\nAn increasing interest in their analyses 把原文里「分析师们的预期」偷换成「对它们\n分析的兴趣增长」，文章并未说人们对植物肉的分析研究兴趣上升。\nB) 无中生有\nAn enormous effort in their promotion 谈的是「推广上的巨大努力」，原文只说市\n场规模会增长，没提企业或政府投入推广，属无中生有。\nC) ✓ 正确\nthe market will skyrocket to $74 billion by 2030—a 957 percent surge ↔\nA huge boost in their consumption\n预判要的是「消费规模大增」，A huge boost in their consumption 正是这个语义，\n锁定 C。\nD) 偷换概念\nA noticeable surge in their varieties 把 surge 的对象从原文的「市场规模」换成「种\n类」，原文仅提到 Newer varieties imitate the look, flavor, and texture of meat，说\n新品种已出现，但没说种类会激增。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2025-06-3-r-52", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 52, "stem": "What do we learn about consumers buying Beyond Burger and Impossible Foods products?", "options": ["A) They are mostly non-vegetarians.", "B) They are mostly animal-rights advocates.", "C) Most of them refrain from eating meat for health reasons.", "D) Most of them eat meatless meat to protect the environment."], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A （细节题）\n【判型】What do we learn about consumers buying Beyond Burger and Impossible Foods\nproducts? 是 learn about + 名词结构，问关于该人群在原文中已写明的属性。\n【拆句】What do we learn about consumers buying Beyond Burger and Impossible Foods\nproducts? 主干 What do we learn about consumers，buying ... 是后置的现在分词短语\n作 consumers 的定语，相当于 who buy ...，结构无陷阱。\n【定位】More than nine out of ten consumers who buy Beyond Burger and Impossible Foods products\nalso eat meat.\n在购买 Beyond Burger 和 Impossible Foods 产品的消费者中，有超过十分之九的人同时\n也吃肉。\n【选项】A) ✓ 正确\nMore than nine out of ten consumers who buy Beyond Burger and Impossible Foods products\nalso eat meat\n↔ They are mostly non-vegetarians\n预判说的「多数也吃肉」正对应「多数非素食者」，A 命中。\nB) 以偏概全\nThey are mostly animal-rights advocates 把动物权利说成消费者的主要身份，但原文\nanimal rights, health, or environmental reasons 是并列的三类动机之一，没有说大多\n数是动物权利倡导者。\nC) 以偏概全\nMost of them refrain from eating meat for health reasons 同样把并列三动机里的\n「健康」拎出来说大多数，且 refrain from eating meat（克制吃肉）与原文 reduce\ntheir meat consumption 程度被夸大。\nD) 以偏概全\nMost of them eat meatless meat to protect the environment 同样犯「三选一拎为\n主因」的错误，无原文比例支撑。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2025-06-3-r-53", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 53, "stem": "What can we conclude about Impossible Foods' products from Joan Solsman's remark?", "options": ["A) They more often appeal to meat-eaters.", "B) They very much resemble animal meat.", "C) Some have become its signature dishes.", "D) Most of them are sold as true fake meat."], "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B （推断题）\n【判型】What can we conclude about Impossible Foods' products from Joan Solsman's\nremark? 中 conclude from ... remark 表示要从她的话里推出关于产品的判断。\n【拆句】What can we conclude about Impossible Foods' products from Joan Solsman's\nremark? 主干 What can we conclude about X from Y，about 引出推断对象（产品），\nfrom 引出推断依据（她的话）；这是固定结构。\n【定位】Maybe the best sign that Impossible Foods has cracked the code to realistic fake meat is that I\ncouldn't stand to take another bite.\n或许 Impossible Foods 成功破解「逼真假肉」密码的最有力信号，就是连我（这个素食\n者）都没法再吃一口。\n【选项】A) 答非所问\n题目要求从她的话推断 Impossible Foods 的「产品本身」特性，They more often\nappeal to meat-eaters 说的是「产品更受肉食者欢迎」，那是 Q52 答案，不是 Q53\n的推断方向。\nB) ✓ 正确\nImpossible Foods has cracked the code to realistic fake meat ↔\nThey very much resemble animal meat\n预判要的是「产品逼真像真肉」，They very much resemble animal meat 直接对应，\n锁 B。\nC) 无中生有\nSome have become its signature dishes 说这些菜成了「招牌菜」，原文只说她试吃\n了 a dish from Impossible Foods，并未说哪些是招牌。\nD) 偷换概念\nMost of them are sold as true fake meat 把 realistic fake meat（逼真的假肉）改成\nsold as true fake meat（作为「真假肉」出售），不仅商业化（销售行为）是原文没有\n的，true 修饰 fake meat 的逻辑也与「产品像真肉」相违。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "推断题"]}, {"id": "2025-06-3-r-54", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 54, "stem": "Why can the habit of eating a plant-based diet lead to better health?", "options": ["A) It frees people from any known link to cancer.", "B) It provides all the nutrients for staying healthy.", "C) It helps maintain normal blood pressure and blood sugar.", "D) It reduces various health risks posed by meat consumption."], "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D （细节题）\n【判型】Why can the habit of eating a plant-based diet lead to better health? 用 Why 提问，\n原文有明确因果链时仍属细节题。\n【拆句】Why can the habit of eating a plant-based diet lead to better health? 中主语是 the\nhabit of doing sth（做某事的习惯）这个抽象名词短语，谓语 lead to 表示导致，结构平\n铺直叙，无陷阱。\n【定位】Not only has red meat been linked to cancer, but studies show that people who ditch meat\nhave lower blood pressure, lower average blood sugar, and lower cholesterol levels.\n不仅红肉被发现与癌症相关，研究还显示放弃吃肉的人血压更低、平均血糖更低、胆固醇水\n平也更低。\n【选项】A) 过度推断\nIt frees people from any known link to cancer 中 frees ... from any known link 表\n示「完全免除任何已知关联」，但原文只说红肉与癌症「被发现相关」（red meat\nbeen linked to cancer），并未说弃肉就能彻底斩断所有癌症关联。\nB) 过度推断\nIt provides all the nutrients for staying healthy 说植物饮食提供「所有保持健康所需\n的营养」，P4 只列了维生素、矿物质、蛋白质、纤维四项，没说「全部」。\nC) 以偏概全\nIt helps maintain normal blood pressure and blood sugar 只提血压和血糖，漏了胆\n固醇和癌症，且 maintain normal 意为「维持正常」，比 lower 表达的「降低」程度\n还弱。\nD) ✓ 正确\nNot only has red meat been linked to cancer, but studies show that people who ditch meat have\nlower blood pressure, lower average blood sugar, and lower cholesterol levels.\n↔ It reduces various health risks posed by meat consumption\n预判要「综合性下降」，D 的 various health risks 正好覆盖了四种风险，是唯一概\n括项。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2025-06-3-r-55", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 55, "stem": "What does the author recommend we eat at the end of the passage?", "options": ["A) Naturally produced foods.", "B) Processed protein substitutes.", "C) Red meats like traditional beef.", "D) New generations of alternate meat."], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A （细节题）\n【判型】What does the author recommend we eat at the end of the passage? 位置写死、动作\n写死，只问被推荐的事物，不涉及推断。\n【拆句】What does the author recommend we eat at the end of the passage? 中 recommend\n(that) we eat 省略 that，we eat 是宾语从句；at the end of the passage 是位置状语，\n结构无陷阱。\n【定位】These new generations of alternate meat get all the attention, but don't forget about whole\ngrains and vegetables. Beans are an especially excellent protein substitute. They're nutritious,\ninexpensive, and far more sustainable than any of the processed meatless substitutes on the\nmarket today.\n这些新一代的肉类替代品备受关注，但不要忘了全谷物和蔬菜。豆类是格外出色的蛋白替代\n品。它们营养丰富、价格便宜，可持续性远胜于今天市面上任何加工过的植物肉替代品。\n【选项】A) ✓ 正确\nwhole grains and vegetables ↔ Naturally produced foods\nfar more sustainable than any of the processed meatless substitutes ↔\nNaturally produced foods\n预判要「天然食物」，Naturally produced foods 同时覆盖三类推荐对象的共同属性，\n是唯一合适项。\nB) 与原文相反\nProcessed protein substitutes 说的正是作者要反对的加工蛋白替代品，far more\nsustainable than any of the processed meatless substitutes 明确把这类产品当对\n照面。\nC) 与原文相反\nRed meats like traditional beef 是全文反复劝退的对象（red meat been linked to\ncancer），作者绝不会在结尾推荐它。\nD) 与原文相反\nNew generations of alternate meat 与 These new generations of alternate meat\n原词复现，但原文用 but 转折表示「它们抢了风头但作者另有所荐」，并非推荐对象。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}], "listeningSharedWith": "2025-06-2"});
